    "## PINN approach\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of solving the PDE numerically, we parameterise the solution $u_\\theta(x, t)$ as a neural network and train it to simultaneously:\n",
    "- **match the sparse observations** (data loss)\n",
    "- **satisfy the wave equation** at a set of randomly sampled collocation points (physics loss)\n",
    "\n",
    "The physical residual is $\\mathcal{R} = \\partial_{tt} u_\\theta - c^2\\, \\partial_{xx} u_\\theta$, which should be zero everywhere in the domain if the network has learned the correct solution."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "731b061d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Sparse observations\n",
    "\n",
    "To simulate a realistic scenario we do not give the PINN the full reference grid. At each of the 100 time steps we randomly draw 1–5 spatial points, giving roughly ~300 observations out of 10 000 grid points (~3%), spread unevenly across space and time.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Note on input normalisation:** the MLP inputs $(x, t)$ are linearly rescaled to $[-1, 1]$ before being passed to the network. Without this, the different magnitudes of $x \\in [0, 5]$ and $t \\in [0, 2]$ can distort early gradient updates and slow convergence."
   ]
